{"id": "section-body-10043", "title": "Bleeding Esophageal Varices", "chapterId": "chapter-ten", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "GASTROINTESTINAL DISEASE CONDITIONS", "Disorders of The Liver and Biliary Tract", "Bleeding Esophageal Varices"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-10043", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Bleeding Esophageal Varices", "runs": [{"text": "Bleeding"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Esophageal"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Varices"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 3, "numberingId": "1531"}}, {"id": "body-10044", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Esophageal varices are collateral veins within the wall of the esophagus that project directly into the lumen. Acute variceal bleeding is a fatal complication in patients with liver cirrhosis. In patients with decompensated liver cirrhosis accompanied by ascites or hepatic encephalopathy, acute variceal bleeding is associated with a high mortality rate.", "runs": [{"text": "Esophageal varices are collateral veins within the wall of the esophagus that project directly into the lumen. Acute variceal bleeding is a fatal complication in patients with liver cirrhosis. In patients with decompensated liver cirrhosis accompanied by ascites or hepatic encephalopathy, acute variceal bleeding is associated with a high mortality rate."}]}, {"id": "body-10046", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Pharmacological Management", "runs": [{"text": "Pharmacological"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Management"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-10047", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "S: octreotide (SC) 50–100µg 8hourly for 3days (infusion 50µg/hour for 72hours up to 5days)", "runs": [{"text": "S:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "octreotide"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(SC)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "50–100µg"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "8hourly"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "for"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "3days"}, {"text